    "https://bellarmine.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=194641": {
      "title": "Bellarmine University \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Bellarmine University\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Health Care Administration & Public Health Bellarmine University \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Health Care Administration & Public Health Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty - Health Care Administration & Public Health Category: Adjunct Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: College of Health Professions-College of Health Professions Locations: Louisville, KY Posted: Nov 10, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Temporary Position ID: 194641 Share About Bellarmine University: Bellarmine University is a dynamic, growing university with a strong liberal arts focus in the Catholic higher education tradition and a bold vision for the future. Bellarmine has undergraduate and graduate enrollment of approximately 3,200 students and continues to add new academic programs while maintaining small class sizes and personal attention to students. Bellarmine is listed in the Princeton Review's Best 387 Colleges and is recognized as a top U.S. university in rankings published by U.S. News & World Report and Forbes. In a survey by The Princeton Review, students praised Bellarmine as a place that is \"welcoming to every single person and makes an effort to include everyone.\" Bellarmine students, faculty and staff engage in more than 25,000 cumulative hours of service each year, in Louisville and around the nation and world. SmartAsset ranked Louisville among the top 25 U.S. cities for best work-life balance, while workplace insights platform kununu.com named Louisville one of the top five U.S. cities for happy employees. Glassdoor recently recognized Louisville as one of the top 15 cities for jobs, based on hiring opportunity, cost of living and job satisfaction. ZipRecruiter calls Louisville a top 10 city for job seekers, thanks in part to its central location and low unemployment. Louisville is within a day's drive of two-thirds of the U.S. population. Deeply embedded in the heart of Louisville, Bellarmine is an engaged partner expanding learning opportunities, enhancing career networks and economic growth, and advancing equity and quality of life for its neighboring communities. By connecting the campus, the community, and the curriculum through engaged scholarship and action, we enhance social and civic engagement, including faculty, staff, and student-led, asset-based initiatives. Bellarmine is an affirmative action and equal opportunity employer and encourages applicants with diverse backgrounds to apply. Job Description: HLTH 331 introduces undergraduate health administration students to the principles and practices of healthcare quality assurance, performance improvement, patient safety, risk management, and regulatory compliance. The course emphasizes leadership competencies, measurement frameworks, continuous improvement models (e.g., PDSA, Lean, Six Sigma), and the integration of ethical, legal, and strategic considerations into quality management systems. Requirements: Education: Doctoral degree (PhD, DNP, DrPH, DHA, DSc, or equivalent) in Health Services Administration, Public Health, Healthcare Quality, Nursing, Business Administration, or a related field with a focus on healthcare management, policy, or systems leadership. Experience: Minimum of five years of professional experience in healthcare quality, performance improvement, patient safety, or regulatory compliance in clinical, administrative, or consulting settings. Demonstrated familiarity with national quality frameworks (e.g., IHI Model for Improvement, CMS Quality Measures, Joint Commission standards, NCQA, AHRQ, or NAHQ CPHQ domains). Prior teaching experience at the undergraduate or graduate level is strongly preferred, particularly in health administration or quality management programs. Professional Competencies: Mastery of quality improvement methodologies (Lean, Six Sigma, Root Cause Analysis, PDSA cycles). Ability to integrate data analytics and performance measurement tools to evaluate quality outcomes. Strong knowledge of risk management, patient safety, and compliance frameworks within both acute and post-acute care environments. Evidence of leadership and collaboration in interdisciplinary healthcare teams. Familiarity with federal and state regulatory standards relevant to healthcare organizations (CMS, OSHA, HIPAA, Joint Commission). Additional Information: Preferred Qualifications: National certification in healthcare quality or patient safety, such as Certified Professional in Healthcare Quality (CPHQ), Certified Professional in Patient Safety (CPPS), or Lean Six Sigma Green/Black Belt. Experience supervising or mentoring students in quality improvement projects or capstone research. Evidence of scholarly engagement (publications, conference presentations, or applied research) in healthcare quality, performance improvement, or safety science. Proficiency with health information systems, data visualization tools, and performance dashboards. Instructional Expectations: Deliver engaging, evidence-based lectures and facilitate discussions that connect theory to real-world healthcare challenges. Design and assess assignments that align with contemporary quality frameworks, including measurement, process mapping, and improvement strategies. Incorporate case studies, benchmarking data, and industry standards to help students analyze and propose quality improvement interventions. Provide timely and constructive feedback, emphasizing leadership, ethics, and professional communication. Foster a classroom environment that supports critical thinking, collaboration, and continuous learning. Utilize Moodle (LMS) and related digital tools to manage instructional content, assignments, and student engagement. Contemporary Context and Alignment: This course reflects current practices in healthcare quality and performance improvement, aligning with key competencies identified by the National Association for Healthcare Quality (NAHQ), including: Performance and Process Improvement Health Data Analytics Patient Safety and Risk Management Regulatory and Accreditation Quality Leadership and Integration Population Health and Care Transitions The instructor should demonstrate the ability to connect these domains to organizational strategy, operational excellence, and leadership development at the undergraduate level, fostering both technical and managerial understanding among future healthcare leaders. Application Instructions: Please submit a cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not provided for this position. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 2001 Newburg Rd, Louisville, KY 40205"
    },
    "https://bellarmine.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203093": {
      "title": "Bellarmine University \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Bellarmine University\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - HLTH 230 Social Determinants of Health Bellarmine University \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - HLTH 230 Social Determinants of Health Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty - HLTH 230 Social Determinants of Health Category: Adjunct Positions Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: College of Health Professions-College of Health Professions Locations: Louisville, KY Posted: Jul 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Exempt Position ID: 203093 Share About Bellarmine University: Bellarmine University is a dynamic, growing university with a strong liberal arts focus in the Catholic higher education tradition and a bold vision for the future. Bellarmine has undergraduate and graduate enrollment of approximately 3,200 students and continues to add new academic programs while maintaining small class sizes and personal attention to students. Bellarmine is listed in the Princeton Review's Best 387 Colleges and is recognized as a top U.S. university in rankings published by U.S. News & World Report and Forbes. In a survey by The Princeton Review, students praised Bellarmine as a place that is \"welcoming to every single person and makes an effort to include everyone.\" Bellarmine students, faculty and staff engage in more than 25,000 cumulative hours of service each year, in Louisville and around the nation and world. SmartAsset ranked Louisville among the top 25 U.S. cities for best work-life balance, while workplace insights platform kununu.com named Louisville one of the top five U.S. cities for happy employees. Glassdoor recently recognized Louisville as one of the top 15 cities for jobs, based on hiring opportunity, cost of living and job satisfaction. ZipRecruiter calls Louisville a top 10 city for job seekers, thanks in part to its central location and low unemployment. Louisville is within a day's drive of two-thirds of the U.S. population. Deeply embedded in the heart of Louisville, Bellarmine is an engaged partner expanding learning opportunities, enhancing career networks and economic growth, and advancing equity and quality of life for its neighboring communities. By connecting the campus, the community, and the curriculum through engaged scholarship and action, we enhance social and civic engagement, including faculty, staff, and student-led, asset-based initiatives. Bellarmine is an affirmative action and equal opportunity employer and encourages applicants with diverse backgrounds to apply. Job Description: Bellarmine University invites applications for a qualified Adjunct Faculty member to teach one section of HLTH 230: Social Determinants of Health during the Fall 2026 semester. This part-time teaching opportunity is ideal for a professional or educator with expertise in public health, population health, healthcare, health equity, child life, or a closely related field. The successful candidate will provide engaging, evidence-based instruction that helps students understand the complex social and structural factors that shape health outcomes. The instructor will encourage students to examine health disparities critically, apply course concepts to real-world situations, and consider strategies for improving health and well-being across diverse populations. Adjunct faculty appointments are made on a semester-by-semester basis and are subject to departmental needs, course enrollment, and university approval. Course Description HLTH 230 provides an interdisciplinary overview of the social determinants of health and their influence on individual, community, and population-level outcomes. The course examines how social, economic, political, educational, racial, cultural, psychosocial, and environmental conditions contribute to differences in health status and access to care. Students explore health disparities associated with healthcare access, affordability, quality, geography, education, income, housing, food security, employment, discrimination, and other social and structural factors. The course also considers the ways in which public policies, institutions, community resources, family systems, and individual experiences affect health and well-being throughout the lifespan. Through readings, discussions, case studies, assignments, and applied learning activities, students will develop a stronger understanding of health equity, population health, and evidence-based approaches to preventing illness and reducing disparities. Students will be encouraged to evaluate current health challenges, consider the experiences of diverse communities, and identify interventions that may improve health outcomes at the individual, organizational, community, and societal levels. This course has been pre-approved by the Association for Child Life Professionals to partially fulfill the academic eligibility requirements for students pursuing the Certified Child Life Specialist credential. The successful candidate will: Teach one section of HLTH 230: Social Determinants of Health during the Fall 2026 semester. Prepare and deliver engaging, organized, and evidence-based instruction in alignment with the approved course description, syllabus, learning outcomes, and departmental expectations. Incorporate current research, real-world examples, case studies, and applied learning opportunities related to public health, health equity, healthcare access, and population health. Foster an inclusive and respectful learning environment that supports open discussion, critical thinking, and the consideration of diverse perspectives and lived experiences. Help students analyze the relationship between social conditions, systemic inequities, public policy, and health outcomes. Facilitate meaningful class discussions and encourage students to apply course concepts to professional, community, and healthcare settings. Evaluate student learning through assignments, discussions, presentations, examinations, projects, and other approved assessment methods. Provide clear expectations, timely and constructive feedback, and consistent communication throughout the semester. Maintain accurate attendance, assignment, and grading records. Submit grades, assessment information, and required course materials by established university deadlines. Hold appropriate office hours or provide another reliable method for students to receive academic support. Use the university's learning management system and other approved instructional technology to post course materials, communicate with students, collect assignments, and maintain grades. Follow applicable university policies related to academic integrity, accessibility, student privacy, classroom conduct, and instructional responsibilities. Work collaboratively with the Program Chair and department faculty to support consistency in course content, academic standards, assessment practices, and student learning outcomes. Notify the Program Chair promptly of significant student concerns, academic issues, or other matters that may affect successful course completion. Requirements: Preferred candidates will possess: A master's degree or higher in public health, health sciences, healthcare administration, sociology, social work, child life, psychology, education, nursing, or a closely related discipline. Professional or academic experience related to social determinants of health, health equity, population health, community health, healthcare access, or health disparities. Previous college-level teaching experience. Experience teaching or mentoring undergraduate students. Demonstrated ability to communicate complex health and social science concepts clearly and effectively. Experience creating inclusive learning environments for students from diverse academic, cultural, and professional backgrounds. Familiarity with learning management systems and instructional technology. Knowledge of the academic requirements associated with Certified Child Life Specialist eligibility is beneficial but not required. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to student learning, academic excellence, inclusive teaching practices, and Bellarmine University's educational mission. Application Instructions: Please submit a cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. Please note: Visa sponsorship is not provided for this position. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation 2001 Newburg Rd, Louisville, KY 40205"
    },
